e had in  purple some of our automated chemistry  hematology and coagulation equipment  that was now going to be incorporated to  our new automation line when we designed  the new facility we knew we wanted to  open for concepts so luckily that worked  to our advantage as we were moving in  this new piece of equipment and  designing where it would be located so  as you'll see in the after pictures we  have this section here that we moved  from east to west north to south and had  minimal changes in the way the  electrical and data and plumbing needed  to be redesigned simply because we've  had the raised floor and it was  conveniently located in the same general  area so there was minimal impact to the  design of  the building for the new placement of  the equipment now we did originally have  designed over here on the left hand side  some semi automated testing and we flip  that down to this bottom section here as  you'll see by by just simply moving some  of the casework that was originally  going to be in the building we also  changed some of the electrical and  plumbing and data but again minimal  impact because of the raised floor made  it very convenient to simply move things  around a little bit so in summary we  have some evolving testing technology  ever changing within the laboratory  environment I know all of you  experienced this unfortunately that can  truly impact us when you have concrete  floor walls that may or may not allow  you the space for you to be able to  introduce a new piece of equipment into  something as simple as a doorway to be  able to get the equipment into the  building or into a specific room really  provides a significant space constraint  that a lot of people struggle to deal  with so we were very fortunate when we  developed the building we now have that  flexible response that as we have these  changes we won't have to have walls  busted down or major construction for  the flooring so that's a benefit to us  and met one of the objectives that we  were looking at when designing the  building we moved away from that  traditional distribution of services  looking up so we don't have to worry  about the eyesore of the various tubing  or plugs that are hanging from the  ceiling we don't have a limitation  from where those columns were from old  spaces that we had to deal with we have  that new approach of looking down at our  raised floor and that flooring allows us  such flexibility that we can really  change as the future brings it to us in  our old building we were literally in  the hundred-year-old portion of the  building and had been there that long so  we would expect that we will spend the  next hundred plus year in our new  building that will allow us such  flexibility that as technology and what  was changed we won't have to worry about  a large expense that we will have to  contribute to making those changes and  then designing flexibility so all of the  opportunities that we have with our  casework makes it possible for us to  change with the work force to our  casework is frequently on wheels so it's  very easy to move and then the race  floor of course adding that  functionality that's easy so not only  does it allow for ease but it also  allows for limited dish disruption and  allowing us to make a quick change as I  had shown you before that receiving and  processing area where we made those  changes all of that was done in less  than a day and if we had been in another  facility or even our old lab space  unfortunately we would have had to have  significant downtime and potential major  construction that would have limited the  schedule and the cost associated with  that I want to take this time to thank  you for having me and open it up for any  questions that you may have  thank you for your informative  presentation Tammy and for such valuable  details we want to get right to the  questions and input from our audience so  here's a reminder for audience members  about how you